The statement is **false**. Here is the justification.

**Claim.** Let $K$ be the smallest subfield of $\mathbb{C}$ containing all roots of unity. Then $\mathbb{C}$ is *not* algebraic over $K$.

**Proof.**

Every root of unity is a root of some $x^n - 1 \in \mathbb{Q}[x]$, hence is algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$. Therefore the field $K$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by all roots of unity is contained in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the field of all algebraic numbers (the algebraic closure of $\mathbb{Q}$ inside $\mathbb{C}$):

$$K \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}.$$

Now $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is countable: there are only countably many polynomials in $\mathbb{Q}[x]$, each with finitely many roots. Hence $K$, being a subfield of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, is also countable.

On the other hand, $\mathbb{C}$ is uncountable. Thus

$$\mathbb{C} \supsetneq \overline{\mathbb{Q}} \supseteq K,$$

so there exist elements of $\mathbb{C}$ that do not lie in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$.

We now show such an element cannot be algebraic over $K$. Suppose $\alpha \in \mathbb{C}$ is algebraic over $K$. Since $K$ is itself algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$ (it is generated by algebraic elements), the tower $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq K \subseteq K(\alpha)$ consists of algebraic extensions; algebraicity is transitive, so $\alpha$ is algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$, i.e. $\alpha \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$.

Contrapositively: any $\alpha \in \mathbb{C} \setminus \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is transcendental over $K$.

Since $\mathbb{C} \setminus \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is nonempty (indeed uncountable), there exist elements of $\mathbb{C}$ transcendental over $K$. Therefore $\mathbb{C}$ is **not** algebraic over $K$.

(Equivalently: $\mathbb{C}$ has positive — in fact uncountable — transcendence degree over $K$, since $K \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and $\mathbb{C}$ has uncountable transcendence degree even over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$.)

